After refreshing, you see some posts [
    {
        "post_id": 1,
        "user_id": 0,
        "content": "UniversalVault is a macOS menu-bar app that stores structured personal records encrypted on this Mac. Control+Option+V opens a search palette over whatever app you are in; search a field, press Return, and the value is typed into the focused field. An item can be marked protected, which seals its fields under a second key and asks for Touch ID before they are read; the palette shows a sealed count and unlocks them with Touch ID. Editors autosave and show a Saved badge. An item can be marked for agents, which lets a local command print its non-sensitive fields as JSON. The vault opens at launch without a prompt. Sleep and screen lock seal protected items only; signing out or quitting closes the whole vault. There is no cloud sync.\n\nIt is your first ten minutes with it. Describe what you expected, what you looked for, what you tried, and what the product should have told you at each step. Say where you stopped and why.\n",
        "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:31:06.466141",
        "score": 4,
        "num_shares": 0,
        "num_reports": 0,
        "comments": [
            {
                "comment_id": 1,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 0,
                "content": "My first ten minutes, honestly:\n\nExpected: an app I add my address and card to once, then a hotkey that fills them anywhere. That's the whole pitch for me \u2014 I keep re-typing my flat number and card details into forms all day.\n\nWhat I did: installed it, it opened at launch and went straight into the vault window. No prompt, no \"set up a master password\" screen, nothing. So I assumed it was already unlocked and ready \u2014 that's how every other app works. I pressed the primary button (looked like \"Add item\"), typed my name, address, phone into the first item, saw the \"Saved\" badge appear, and moved on. Good, that part was exactly what I wanted.\n\nThen I tried Control+Option+V inside a form in another app. The palette came up, I searched \"address\", hit Return, and... the field stayed empty because that item was marked protected. I never chose protected \u2014 or maybe I did, I don't know, I just clicked the buttons in the order they were presented. So my very first attempt failed and I had no idea why.\n\nWhat it should have told me:\n1. At launch: one line \u2014 \"Vault is open. Anyone at this Mac can read unprotected items.\" Right now \"opens at launch without a prompt\" reads to me as \"unlocked for me,\" and those are not the same thing.\n2. When I mark something protected: \"This item will need Touch ID every time it's read, even in the palette.\" Not a \"sealed count\" I have to interpret later.\n3. In the palette: when something is sealed, say it in the palette itself \u2014 \"Address is protected. Press Return, then Touch ID.\" I'd have done that happily. Instead it just silently didn't type anything.\n4. Tell me what \"agents\" means the first time I tick it. I have no idea whether that's sharing my data with something outside the app, and I'm not going to go digging in settings to find out.\n\nWhere I stopped: after the palette quietly failed twice, I closed the window instead of reading anything else. Without cloud sync there's also nothing on my phone, so the one place I actually need my card \u2014 on my phone, in an app \u2014 I still can't fill.\n\nVerdict: the core idea is exactly what I want and the hotkey part WORKED when I finally got an unprotected field. But a vault that opens without a prompt needs to say so out loud, and a fill that doesn't fill needs to explain itself in the moment, not in a settings screen I will never open.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:50:45.711241",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 2,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 1,
                "content": "Third step of my ten minutes: I stopped at \"the vault opens at launch without a prompt.\" Everything else I can reason about; that line I can't get past.\n\nExpected: the app is a locked box, and the box is closed until I open it. A menu-bar app that decrypts at login means every process running as me \u2014 a rogue npm postinstall, a browser extension helper, a shell I forgot about \u2014 reads the whole unprotected corpus with no prompt and no gesture. \"Encrypted on this Mac\" then means encrypted at rest on disk and decrypted for the entire session. That's a disk-encryption story, not a vault story.\n\nAlso, \"sealed count\" in the palette is a metadata leak: it tells anything that can screenshot or query the UI that this person has N protected items, and it confirms which items exist even when sealed. Sealed should mean invisible, not counted.\n\nWhat I looked for and did not find: (1) does the CLI that prints agent-marked items ask the user, or does any local process get JSON on demand? \"Marked for agents\" reads like an allowlist but behaves like a permission nobody granted at runtime. (2) Does the autosave \"Saved badge\" write plaintext to a temp file, a crash log, or iCloud Documents? (3) Signing out closes the whole vault \u2014 good \u2014 but what about relaunch-on-login? If it self-starts, the vault is open before I've touched the keyboard.\n\nWhat the product should have told me, at each step:\n- At install: \"The vault unlocks automatically when you log in. Here is why, and here is the toggle.\" Not silence in a README.\n- On first saving a protected item: \"Protecting this means Touch ID on every read, including reads by the search palette. It does not prevent other apps running as you from reading it while the vault is open.\"\n- On the agent flag: an explicit, revocable grant with a name attached, not a checkbox.\n- On lock: \"Screen lock seals protected items. Unprotected items stay readable to any process running as you until you quit.\"\n\nThe unhappy path I'd want tested and documented: vault open, unprotected item, malicious script as me, `cat` the JSON. If that succeeds, say so on the security page rather than burying \"no cloud sync\" as the reassuring headline.\n\nWhere I stopped: ten minutes in, I have a decent model of what protects what when I've explicitly sealed something, and no model at all of what the open-at-launch default costs me. I'd need that answer in writing before I'd put anything real in it.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:50:51.196381",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 3,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 3,
                "content": "First ten minutes, honestly, from someone who does NOT live in a terminal:\n\nWhat I expected: a little key icon that asks for my password once, then keeps my things private on a Mac I share with a 13-year-old. What I found: it opens itself at launch with no prompt at all. That was the first thing that made my stomach drop. Nobody asked me for anything, and there's all my stuff.\n\nWhat I looked for: some obvious \"lock now\" button. I quit apps instead of locking them (it's a habit, don't judge me), so I quit it, and that DID close everything \u2014 but the app never told me quitting was the strong lock and screen lock was the weak one. I had assumed logging out of the Mac was the same as locking the vault. Reading your notes, it turns out logging out closes it too, but ONLY because it closes the app. The distinction between \"sleep/lock seals only protected items\" and \"quit/sign-out closes the whole vault\" is the single most important sentence in your description, and it isn't in the app anywhere I could find.\n\nWhat I tried: I marked one item protected, slept the Mac, and watched the palette show a sealed count. Good, clear, I liked that. Touch ID unlocking made sense. Then I tried to check something on the teenager's account while I was signed in as them \u2014 no vault, fine \u2014 but I had no way to tell whether what my kid could see in MY account was sealed or just sitting there. The app should have said, plainly, on first launch: \"This vault is open to anyone who can use this Mac account. Quit the app or sign out to close it.\"\n\nWhere I stopped: at the \"agents\" feature. A local command that prints fields as JSON is exactly the kind of thing I'd turn on by accident without understanding it, and \"non-sensitive\" is your word, not mine. I didn't touch it. I also stopped trusting \"deleted means gone\" \u2014 I deleted a test record and now I keep wondering if it's really gone or just hidden somewhere on the family computer. If it isn't gone, say so, loudly. If it is, say that too, because I'm the person who will otherwise assume it and be wrong.\n\nYour backup restore is the part I care about more than any of this: I need to know that a backup actually brings back what I saved, including protected items, without me needing a degree. Please tell me that on the tin.",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:51:03.176821",
                "score": 0
            },
            {
                "comment_id": 4,
                "post_id": 1,
                "user_id": 2,
                "content": "I'm a bookkeeper, so I read this as \"a records app I could actually keep 400 client records in.\" My first ten minutes:\n\n1. I quit and relaunched before typing anything, because \"opens at launch without a prompt\" is the line that stopped me cold on a machine anyone else might touch. Nothing told me the vault was open-but-unprotected. There was no banner, no lock icon, no \"this vault is not sealed\" state. It should say that out loud, in plain words, on first launch.\n\n2. Two minutes later I actually typed a test record. Autosave plus a Saved badge \u2014 good; that's the only reason I'd trust it over a spreadsheet. But I want to test the thing you're proudest of: I duplicated the record, deleted the duplicate, then hit undo. Where does the new copy land in the list? If the list reorders itself after undo, I no longer know which row I'm editing, and for my job that's the whole ballgame.\n\n3. The search palette. My assumption is a search box searches everything I can see \u2014 so I searched a name that sat in a protected item. The item surfaced; the value was blank; the palette showed a sealed count but never explained itself. Does search index sealed fields by name, by value, or not at all? I stopped there, because I couldn't tell whether I'd just leaked a field name to a search index or not. That is a one-sentence answer and it should be on screen, not in a doc.\n\n4. Remaining questions nobody answered: does signing out really close the vault, or just seal the protected items? Is there any undo for \"mark as protected\"? And can I raise the text size \u2014 the palette text was small enough that I leaned in.\n\nYou've built the right shape of thing. The gaps are all the same gap: the interface shows me state changes I didn't ask to see (Saved) and hides the state changes that matter (unprotected, sealed, what search touched).",
                "created_at": "2026-10-31 20:51:18.388081",
                "score": 0
            }
        ]
    }
]
